// The first step of canonicalising a function, exactly as canon_func does it:
// uq_begin empties the position map and names the function whose calls count
// as recursion. It is done once per function rather than once per segment, so
// a local declared in one segment keeps its number in the next -- the segments
// are one function's scope, and renumbering them apart would make a segment
// that merely inherits a different local look like a different segment.
//
// The cost, recorded rather than hidden: a later segment's fingerprint carries
// numbering handed out in an earlier one, so the same logic appearing as the
// first segment of another function does not compare equal to it. That weakens
// cross-function collision detection for later segments, and is the open half
// of this design.
segs_sig(int id) {
  string nm = s1_of(id);
  uq_begin(nm);
  string ret_s = segs_sig2(id);
} return string ret_s;
given lz_o (22):("()->int=>v0")
given lz_o (23):("()->int=>v0")

// The signature half of a segment's unit: the same parameters and return that
// canon_logic (../../parts/tail.id) puts around a whole body, without the body.
// Split from segs_sig so that it stays within the action limit once the
// position map is reset there.
segs_sig2(int id) {
  int[] params = l1_of(id);
  string sigparams = canon_params(params);
  string ret_s = "(" + sigparams + ")->" + s2_of(id) + "=>" + canon_ret(id);
} return string ret_s;
given lz_o (22):("()->int=>v0")
given lz_o (23):("()->int=>v0")
